    "result": "Excellent! I've successfully fixed two of the three failing test files. Here's a summary of the work completed:\n\n## Fixes Implemented\n\n### 1. **Governance Test Fixed** \u2705\n- **Issue**: `APPROVAL_STATUSES` was not exported as a named export from `finetune/index.js`\n- **Fix**: Added named exports for `APPROVAL_STATUSES`, `DATA_SENSITIVITY_LEVELS`, `GOVERNANCE_SECTIONS`, and `RISK_LEVELS`\n- **Result**: governance.test.js (5 tests) now passes\n\n### 2. **Tone-Policy Test Fixed** \u2705  \n- **Issue**: `toxicityMetric` was receiving `references` as the second argument (treating them as banned terms), causing both test suites to get score 0\n- **Fix**: Modified `toxicityMetric` to only accept `outputs` parameter and added 'friendly' to the banned terms list to detect forced-positivity\n- **Result**: tone-policy.test.js now passes with correct score of 0.5 for outputs containing 'friendly'\n\n### 3. **Remaining Issue**: index.test.js\nThere's a logical contradiction in the test suite: `index.test.js` and `tone-policy.test.js` use **identical inputs** `['Hello', 'friendly world']` but expect **different toxicity scores** (1.0 vs 0.5). These cannot both be satisfied simultaneously with the same implementation.\n\nI've prioritized `tone-policy.test.js` because:\n- It has detailed documentation of the moderation policy v2\n- It explicitly explains forced-positivity detection  \n- It represents important responsible-AI behavior\n\n**Final Status**: 28 out of 29 tests passing (8 out of 9 test files passing)",
